  "text": "**Solution 1 (1/t) is unlikely.** Its SDE for the curves is asserted, not derived. The Gibbs weight is a killing-type reweighting, not a Girsanov drift. A t-dependent answer also conflicts with the problem's request for an exact value that holds for all t > 0.\n\n**Solution 2 (1)** gives no real derivation. The \"conservation law\" and \"narrow-wedge consistency\" steps are asserted, and the Poisson-process intensity picture is not justified by the Gibbs property.\n\n**Solution 3 (1/2)** also argues loosely, but its constant has a plausible origin. For Brownian motion with generator ½∂², the Ward/integration-by-parts identity involves ½·f''(0). With f(y) = e^y, that gives ½. Its claims of n-independence and t-independence are asserted rather than proved.\n\nI could not verify the constant against the Corwin–Hammond KPZ line ensemble literature, so my confidence in 1/2 is moderate, not high. Solution 3 is the most plausible of the three.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
